// A constant EXPRESSION (not a bare integer literal) reaching an annotated
// `let` is range-checked against the declared width, exactly as a bare literal
// of the same value is. `1 << 20` is 1048576, out of `UInt16`'s 0..=65535
// range, so it is a compile error — where it used to compile clean and print
// `0` (1048576 mod 65536), a different in-range value, silently (DF-235b).
//
// SAME RULE, one funnel, at every fixed-width position it serves: a `static`
// initializer, a struct-literal field, a positional argument, `return`, a
// default parameter value, a value `if` arm, a `match` arm, and a plain
// assignment target. `_apply_literal_expected_type` gained the const-expression
// case, folds through the one `const_eval`, and range-checks the result where
// it range-checks a literal — so none of those positions carries a rule of its
// own. The out-of-range value is pinned once, here.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: does not fit in `UInt16`

func main() {
    let e: UInt16 = 1 << 20
    print("{e}")
}
